% ------------------------------------------------------------- findings ----
\section*{Summary of findings}

\small
\noindent
Every figure below is computed, not quoted, and every table it rests on is
complete: an exact distance for every legal position, both sides to move.
Section numbers point at the argument.

\smallskip
\noindent\textbf{Depth obeys a law, except when it does not.}\ The deepest KQK
win on an $n\times n$ board is mate in $\lceil 3(n-2)/2\rceil$, with no
exception over $9 \le n \le 60$ and the ordinary chessboard as the sole one
($10$ rather than $9$). KRK has no expression of that shape at all: what holds
is a recurrence of period six, and even that fails once, at $n=24$. KBNK is not
linear but \emph{quadratic}, and zig-zags with the parity of the board.
\hfill(\S\ref{sec:depth})

\smallskip
\noindent\textbf{Square colour decides two endgames outright.}\ Two bishops of
one colour can never mate --- the program computes that rather than assuming
it, and the drawn positions of KBBK are exactly the same-coloured ones. On an
\emph{odd} board every corner is the same colour, and that single fact is why
KBNK's depths alternate.\hfill(\S\ref{sec:colour})

\smallskip
\noindent\textbf{A queen needs no table.}\ Six rules, a pure function of the
position with no lookup and no dependence on $n$, mate from \emph{every} legal
position on every board from $4\times4$ to $14\times14$ --- 13\,559\,524 of
them --- taking at worst $1.79$ times as long as perfect play.\hfill(\S\ref{sec:rule})

\smallskip
\noindent\textbf{Arm the defender and the win can vanish.}\ KQKR has the
steepest growth here and it never settles: 35, 44, 54, 69, 85, 108, 132 moves
for $n = 8\ldots14$, then \textbf{218} at $n=15$ --- and at $n=16$ a whole class
of positions stops being winnable at all, the drawn fraction jumping from
$0.13\,\%$ to $26.6\,\%$ while the deepest win \emph{falls}.\hfill(\S\ref{sec:kqkr})

\smallskip
\noindent\textbf{Two black kings are easier to mate than one.}\ Give Black a
second king, with the rule that a mate counts only when both are mated at once,
and White wins \emph{every} legal position at every board size from 3 to 17.
The $8\times8$ mate is in \textbf{7} moves against 10 for the same queen
against a single king, because the winning method is a fork rather than a
corner: in $56.8\,\%$ of the mates the white king contributes nothing at all,
and in $26.9\,\%$ neither black king is even on the rim. The rule's price is
93\,508 placements on $8\times8$ that the ordinary rule would call mate, and
there is no positional draw anywhere in the table --- every drawn position is a
stalemate or a queen hanging.\hfill(\S\ref{sub:kqkk-graph}--7.4)

\smallskip
\noindent\textbf{Play for the kings instead and it becomes a fight.}\ Replace
mate by the capture it stands for --- every king an ordinary man, and the game
won by taking all of the opponent's --- and the same four men give White
$65.9\,\%$ of the placements the
mating rule gives him $100\,\%$ of, with Black holding a third and winning a
few. Two things carry it, and both are computed: \textbf{two kings hunt down a
lone king from every position on every board up to $14\times14$}, which is what
makes $\ldots$K$\times$Q fatal rather than drawing, and K\,+\,Q vs K with no
stalemate to dodge is a win everywhere and a move faster than ordinary KQK.
The first of those has a \textbf{critical board size}: at $15\times15$ the hunt
stops working, 93\,\% of the placements it used to win turn drawn, and the
deepest win drops from 62 moves to 37 and stays there to $n=22$.
White's share \emph{grows} with the board, $19.6\,\%$ at $n=4$ to $79.1\,\%$ at
$n=11$. And the rules are a conservative change to the ordinary game rather
than a new one: \textbf{with one king a side they are chess with a single
clause replaced}, under which stalemate becomes a loss --- except in the
totally immobile positions, where a \emph{checkmate} becomes a draw
instead.\hfill(\S\ref{sec:capture}, \S\ref{sub:cap-onceking})

\smallskip
\noindent\textbf{Take the queen away too, and three kings beat two.}\ With
nothing on the board but kings, White wins from \emph{every} white-to-move
placement on every board from $3\times3$ to $11\times11$ --- $1\,987\,925\,940$
of them at $n=11$ --- and the whole endgame turns on one row of the census: three
kings against one is won with \emph{either} side to move, so any capture White
can make wins on the spot. It is the only table here in which material falls on
\emph{both} sides, and neither player is ever worse off for having to move. On
the two smallest boards the extra king is worth nothing without the move:
\textbf{404} placements on $4\times4$ are a White win with White to play and a
\emph{Black} win with Black to play. The deepest win doubles its step in five
board sizes, from $+10$ to $+24$ a board --- and the clause the whole thing
rests on expires at $15\times15$, where both endgames it converts into collapse
at once, three boards past where the table can still be built.\hfill(\S\ref{sec:kings})

\smallskip
\noindent\textbf{``Two knights cannot mate'' is false as stated, and it costs a
table.}\ K+N+N vs K holds \textbf{120} mates on $8\times8$ --- exactly $16n-8$
on every board from $4\times4$ up. What is true is that Black is never in
\emph{zugzwang}: the deepest white win is \textbf{one ply} at every size from 3
to 16, so White wins only where he mates on the move. That is why three knights
cannot score $\ldots$K$\times$N as a draw and need a KNNK table underneath them
--- the only converting endgame here whose sub-table a careless reading would
have assumed away. Three knights then win $98.7\,\%$ of $8\times8$ placements
rather than all of them, and \textbf{nine drawn positions in ten have a knight
hanging} against one won position in eight.\hfill(\S\ref{sec:knights})

\smallskip
\noindent\textbf{One clause turns a drawn endgame into a won one.}\ Under the
capture rules of \S\ref{sec:capture}, which for two knights against a bare king reduce
\emph{exactly} to scoring stalemate as a loss, K\,+\,N\,+\,N vs K goes from
616 won placements on $8\times8$ to \textbf{5\,713\,976} --- a factor of
9\,276 --- and up to $6\times6$ White wins \emph{every} legal position. Two
knights cannot force mate; they can force stalemate. And then it has a critical
board size of its own: the deepest win climbs 51, 61, 75, 91, 111, 151,
\textbf{219} plies for $n = 8\ldots14$ and the win then collapses to
$0.45\,\%$ at $15\times15$. A lone knight does the same thing sooner, winning
$99.5\,\%$ of $6\times6$ placements and a fixed 2\,900 on every board from
$10\times10$ up.\hfill(\S\ref{sub:kn-capture}--9.3)

\smallskip
\noindent\textbf{What the cross-checks were for.}\ Three of the bugs they
caught were not crashes but plausible numbers. A successor counter, the
textbook way to run the induction, is \emph{unsound} on a symmetry-reduced
index, and this program does without one. Treating ``the king steps onto a
piece'' as a capture is sound with one white piece and wrong with two: it cost
KBNK two moves of depth on $8\times8$ and nothing at all in KBBK, where it hid
for as long as KBBK existed. And a table that \emph{converts} cannot be swept
until nothing changes, nor can a value be taken as soon as it is
computable.\hfill(\S\ref{sec:trust})

\normalsize
\vspace{3mm}
