\noindent{\small The first is a fortress whose depth runs away with the board
until the win disappears. The second is a queen mating two kings by herself in
the open, on two rays at once, with her king in the far corner. The third is
what ``both at once'' costs: Black has no move and one king is in check, and
this rule declines to call it a mate. The fourth has no counterpart in ordinary
chess at all --- a queen on the board, nothing hanging, and White is lost,
because two kings that guard each other have sealed his king in the corner. The
fifth is the whole board: five kings, nine squares, and White mates in 3 with
the move while Black mates in 5 without it.}

