\section{The endgames}\label{sec:endgames}

Black has a lone king in the first four. White has one or two long-range men.
Every table below is complete: an exact distance to mate for every legal
position on the board, for both sides to move. The middle two do not leave
Black bare and are set apart for it: the fifth gives Black a rook and obeys none
of what follows (\S\ref{sec:kqkr}), while the sixth gives Black a second \emph{king}, which
obeys all of it and is the easiest endgame here --- until the rule that a mate
must catch both kings is replaced by the capture it stands for, at which point
the same four men break every one of the invariants below and Black can win
(\S\ref{sec:kqkk}).

The last two are set apart for the opposite reason. Black is bare in both, as
bare as he is in the first four, and it is \emph{White's} material that breaks
the rule: three knights are the one holding here from which a capture does not
end the game, because the two left over can still mate (\S\ref{sec:knights}).

\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}llrrr@{}}
\toprule
& \textbf{White has} & \textbf{$8\times8$ deepest} & \textbf{largest $n$} & \textbf{entries/side there} \\
\midrule
{\color{kqk}$\blacksquare$}~\textbf{KQK}  & king and queen            & mate in 10 & 60 & 5\,820\,822\,000 \\
{\color{krk}$\blacksquare$}~\textbf{KRK}  & king and rook             & mate in 16 & 54 & 3\,092\,041\,836 \\
{\color{kbbk}$\blacksquare$}~\textbf{KBBK}& king and two bishops      & mate in 19 & 22 & 3\,375\,083\,250 \\
{\color{kbnk}$\blacksquare$}~\textbf{KBNK}& king, bishop and knight   & mate in 33 & 19 & 2\,093\,606\,865 \\
\midrule
{\color{kqkr}$\blacksquare$}~\textbf{KQKR}& king and queen \emph{against king and rook}
                                                                      & mate in 35 & 17 & \phantom{0}856\,925\,460 \\
{\color{kqkk}$\blacksquare$}~\textbf{KQKK}& king and queen \emph{against two kings}
                                                                      & mate in \phantom{0}7 & 24 & 6\,667\,930\,368 \\
\midrule
{\color{knnk}$\blacksquare$}~\textbf{KNNK} & king and two knights     & mate in \phantom{0}1 & 16 & \phantom{00}260\,467\,200 \\
{\color{knnnk}$\blacksquare$}~\textbf{KNNNK}& king and three knights  & mate in 21 & 15 & 11\,591\,361\,600 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\begin{result}[title={The invariant that makes them tractable}]{ink}
Every capture Black can make leaves material that \emph{cannot mate}, so it is
an immediate and permanent draw:
\;$\ldots$K$\times$Q and $\ldots$K$\times$R leave bare kings,
$\ldots$K$\times$B in KBBK leaves KBK,
and in KBNK $\ldots$K$\times$B leaves KNK while $\ldots$K$\times$N leaves KBK.

Consequently no position ever has a predecessor outside its own table: the
unmove generator needs \textbf{no un-captures at all}, and one self-contained
table per endgame suffices.

KNK and KNNK join them, and KNNK is the sharper case: $\ldots$K$\times$N leaves
KNK, in which \textbf{no mate exists on any board size}, so the capture is not
merely a draw by best play but dead.

\medskip
\textbf{This is exactly where it stops,} and knights mark the boundary more
finely than rooks do. KRRK looks like KBBK and is not --- taking one rook leaves
KRK, which White \emph{wins}. KNNNK looks like KBBK too, and is also not, but
only just: taking one knight leaves KNNK, which White wins from 1\,448 positions
on a $16\times16$ board out of $1.9$ billion. Rare is not never, and \S\ref{sec:knights} is what
that costs. Piece count is not the criterion; what matters is that \emph{Black}
stays bare and that every capture is drawn. \S\ref{sec:kqkr} crosses the line deliberately, to find out what the restriction
was costing: the answer is a factor of $n$.

\smallskip
\S\ref{sec:kqkk} shows that ``bare'' is the wrong word for it. Give Black a second
\emph{king} and every clause above still holds --- it cannot check, it cannot
be taken, and $\ldots$K$\times$Q is still an immediate draw --- so the whole
apparatus survives a man being added to Black. What breaks it is Black being
able to \emph{do} something, not Black having something --- and \S\ref{sec:capture} does
exactly that without moving a man, by playing for the kings themselves instead
of for mate.
\end{result}

\vspace{2mm}
