\end{minipage}

\smallskip

\noindent
Read the six boards in order and the method is plain. The kings advance
\emph{abreast}, never more than a square apart, so that the white king can
never take one without being taken by the other: it is that mutual guard, not
the number of men, that does the work. White runs from the centre to the edge
and then along it, and each time he shuffles the pair closes up one more file.
The last board is the point of the whole rule set --- 16$\ldots$Kf7-g7 puts a
black king \emph{beside} the white one, which no mating rule in this document
would allow, and White cannot take it because the second king covers g7. He has
only h8, and 17$\ldots$Kg7$\times$h8 ends the game.

\subsection{The endgame that results, and a queen that does not save White}\label{sub:cap-queen}

Counted over every placement, two kings that may walk up to anything leave a
great deal hanging, and the census says more about the tempo than about the
endgame. Restricting to \emph{quiet} placements --- no black king attacked by
the queen, none beside the white king, which is exactly the set the mating rule
calls legal --- gives the honest comparison, over the same 2\,534\,392
placements:

\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrr@{}}
\toprule
$8\times8$, quiet placements & White wins & draw & Black wins \\
\midrule
mating rule, white to move   & \textbf{100\,\%} & 0 & 0 \\
capture rule, white to move  & 65.9\,\% & 34.1\,\% & 48 placements \\
capture rule, black to move  & 44.9\,\% & 55.0\,\% & 0.1\,\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\noindent
Black's third of the board is the phalanx holding. He seldom \emph{wins} from a
quiet placement --- he needs something hanging first --- but he draws by keeping
his kings together and his eye on the queen. The 48 that White loses with the
move and nothing hanging are zugzwangs, and they all look like this:

\begin{center}
\begin{minipage}{0.34\textwidth}\centering
  \bdw{4.2cm}{8}{\pw{0}{0}{\glyphK}\pw{7}{2}{\glyphQ}\pb{2}{0}\pb{2}{1}}\\[1.5mm]
  {\small\color{kqkk}White to move, \textbf{lost} in 4 plies}
\end{minipage}\hspace{0.03\textwidth}
\begin{minipage}{0.58\textwidth}
\small
Nothing is hanging. Kings on $c1$ and $c2$ cover $a2$, $b1$ and $b2$ between
them and guard each other, so the white king can neither move nor take; the
queen is on the far side and no move of hers stops $\ldots$K--$b2$ and
$\ldots$K$\times a1$. \textbf{White has a queen on the board and is lost
because his king is sealed in the corner by two kings that defend each
other} --- a shape with no counterpart in ordinary chess.

\smallskip
The tempo can decide the game outright. With wK\,$a1$, wQ\,$h1$ against kings
$g1$ and $a8$: White to move plays Q$\times g1$ and wins in 6 moves; Black to
move plays $\ldots$K$\times h1$ and wins in 18.
\end{minipage}
\end{center}

\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrrrrrr@{}}
\toprule
$n$ & 3 & 4 & 5 & 6 & 7 & 8 & 9 & 10 & 11 \\
\midrule
deepest \textbf{white} win (moves) & 4 & 6 & 9 & 10 & 12 & 13 & 15 & 16 & 18 \\
deepest \textbf{black} win (moves) & 3 & 6 & 10 & 12 & 15 & 18 & 24 & 29 & 36 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\noindent
White's depth grows like the mating rule's, roughly doubled: catch a king, then
mate it. Black's grows faster, and the deepest example says why --- his win is
$\ldots$K$\times$Q followed by a hunt, and a hunt across an $n\times n$ board is
what costs the extra moves.

\medskip
\noindent
This rule set breaks all four of the structural facts in \S\ref{sec:endgames} at once, with the
same four men on the board: Black can check, White can capture, no capture is
drawn, and the table converts three ways rather than none. It therefore has its
own solver --- signed entries, three sub-endgames built in dependency order, and
an induction that runs on buckets keyed by depth rather than in alternation,
because a conversion can name any depth and the plies in between may be empty.

\subsection{The three rule sets, in numbers}\label{sub:cap-three}

The same four men, read three ways. Each rule calls a different set of
placements legal --- the mating rules bar a black king from standing beside the
white king or in the queen's line, the capture rules bar nothing --- so the
counts below are over different sets, and the row that compares like with like
is the third, which restricts the capture table to the placements the strict
rule admits.

\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrr@{}}
\toprule
$8\times8$, white to move & placements & White wins & draw & Black wins & deepest \\
\midrule
mating rule, strict          & 2\,534\,392 & 2\,534\,392 & 0 & --- & 7 moves \\
                             &             & (100\,\%)   & & & \\
mating rule, loose           & 5\,481\,620 & 544 & 5\,481\,076 & --- & 2 moves \\
                             &             & (0.01\,\%) & (99.99\,\%) & & \\
capture rule, quiet only     & 2\,534\,392 & 1\,670\,492 & 863\,852 & 48 & 13 moves \\
                             &             & (65.9\,\%) & (34.1\,\%) & & \\
capture rule, all placements & 7\,624\,512 & 6\,185\,496 & 1\,419\,192 & 19\,824 & 13 moves \\
                             &             & (81.1\,\%) & (18.6\,\%) & (0.3\,\%) & \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\noindent
Under the mating rules Black is either lost everywhere or safe almost
everywhere, depending on which reading of ``both at once'' is taken. Under the
capture rule he is neither: he holds a third of the board and he can win.

