\noindent
White's share climbs steadily with the board, which is the opposite of what a
bigger board usually does to a defender: the more room there is, the longer it
takes the two black kings to reach anything, and the more of it the queen can
use to separate them before they do. Black's outright \emph{wins} do not follow
it at all --- they are a fixed handful of corner zugzwangs, 12 of them at
$n=4$ and 64 at $n=11$, against 48 million placements at $n=11$.

\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrrrrr@{}}
\toprule
$n$ & 4 & 5 & 6 & 7 & 8 & 9 & 10 & 11 \\
\midrule
White wins, quiet (\%)   & 19.6 & 34.5 & 48.4 & 58.6 & 65.9 & 71.4 & 75.7 & 79.1 \\
drawn, quiet (\%)        & 79.4 & 65.5 & 51.6 & 41.4 & 34.1 & 28.6 & 24.3 & 20.9 \\
