\noindent
The last two rows are the two halves of every win in this endgame. White's
depth is the smaller of them plus the manoeuvring it takes to force a capture;
Black's is dominated by the other, since his win is $\ldots$K$\times$Q and then
a hunt, and the hunt is what grows.

\subsection{Both deepest wins, played out to the capture}\label{sub:cap-deepest}

Neither line ends in a mate, because under these rules there is no mate. They
end with a king taken off the board.

\begin{center}
\begin{minipage}{0.3\textwidth}\centering
  \bdw{3.8cm}{8}{\pw{0}{0}{\glyphK}\pw{0}{1}{\glyphQ}\pb{2}{0}\pb{4}{4}}\\[1.2mm]
  {\small\color{kqkk}white to move,\\\textbf{White wins, 13 moves}}
\end{minipage}\hspace{0.06\textwidth}
\begin{minipage}{0.3\textwidth}\centering
  \bdw{3.8cm}{8}{\pw{0}{0}{\glyphK}\pw{7}{0}{\glyphQ}\pb{6}{0}\pb{0}{7}}\\[1.2mm]
  {\small\color{kqkk}black to move,\\\textbf{Black wins, 18 moves}}
\end{minipage}
\end{center}

\noindent\textbf{wK\,$a1$, wQ\,$a2$ against kings $c1$ and $e5$}, White to
move --- the deepest white win on the ordinary board, 25 plies.

{\small\ttfamily
1.~Qa2-f2 kc1-d1\quad 2.~Ka1-b2 ke5-e6\quad 3.~Kb2-c3 ke6-e7\quad 4.~Kc3-c4 ke7-e8\\
5.~Qf2-e3 ke8-f8\quad 6.~Qe3-f3 kf8-g8\quad 7.~Qf3\underline{x}d1 kg8-f7\quad 8.~Kc4-d5 kf7-g7\\
9.~Kd5-e5 kg7-h7\quad 10.~Ke5-f6 kh7-h8\quad 11.~Kf6-g6 kh8-g8\quad 12.~Qd1-d8 kg8-h8\\
13.~Qd8\underline{x}h8
}

\smallskip
\noindent
Six moves of manoeuvring and not one check: White is not chasing either king,
he is keeping them apart --- they run in opposite directions and never get to
stand together. Only when the $d1$ king is alone on its side of the board is
Q$\times d1$ safe, and the second half is the K\,+\,Q vs K table walking the
survivor into the corner and taking it. \textbf{Thirteen moves, two captures,
no mate.}

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{wK\,$a1$, wQ\,$h1$ against kings $g1$ and $a8$}, Black to
move --- the deepest black win, 35 plies. The queen is standing next to a king,
and it is Black's turn.

{\small\ttfamily
1...~kg1\underline{x}h1\quad 2.~Ka1-b2 kh1-h2\quad 3.~Kb2-c2 kh2-g3\quad 4.~Kc2-d2 kg3-f4\\
5.~Kd2-d3 ka8-b7\quad 6.~Kd3-c4 kb7-c6\quad 7.~Kc4-d4 kc6-d6\quad 8.~Kd4-c3 kd6-c5\\
9.~Kc3-d3 kf4-f3\quad 10.~Kd3-c2 kf3-e3\quad 11.~Kc2-c3 ke3-e2\quad 12.~Kc3-b3 ke2-d3\\
13.~Kb3-a4 kd3-c4\quad 14.~Ka4-a3 kc4-c3\quad 15.~Ka3-a2 kc5-b4\quad 16.~Ka2-a1 kb4-a3\\
17.~Ka1-b1 ka3-b2\quad 18.~Kb1-c1 kb2\underline{x}c1
}

\smallskip
\noindent
One capture and then seventeen moves of pursuit. The far king walks over from
$a8$ to join the near one --- moves 5 to 8 are the two of them forming up ---
and from there they advance together, each covering the other, until the white
king has nowhere left. \textbf{That is the whole reason the queen cannot be
given away.}

\begin{center}\small\color{inksoft}
The data behind all of this:\quad
\texttt{artifacts/results/kqkk-capture.csv}\quad$\cdot$\quad
\texttt{artifacts/stats/kqkkcap*.txt}\\
the two mating readings:\quad
\texttt{kqkk-max-dtm.csv}\quad$\cdot$\quad\texttt{kqkk-loose.csv}
\end{center}

\section{When there is nothing on the board but kings}\label{sec:kings}

\noindent
Delete the queen from \S\ref{sec:capture} and the rules do not change: they were
written for any number of kings a side, and with the queen gone they still are.
What is left is an endgame with no pieces in it at all --- \textbf{three white
kings against two black ones} --- and it is the only table in this document in
which material falls on \emph{both} sides. Every other endgame here converts in
one direction: Black takes a white piece and the game is over or drawn. Here
either side may take, neither capture ends anything, and $(w,b)$ becomes
$(w,b-1)$ when White takes and $(w-1,b)$ when Black does. Six tables, built up a
lattice rather than a chain:
\[
  (1,1) \;\longrightarrow\; (2,1),\,(1,2) \;\longrightarrow\; (3,1),\,(2,2)
        \;\longrightarrow\; (3,2).
\]

\S\ref{sub:cap-stalemates}'s corollary still applies, one man further along.
Immobility needs every square beside every one of a side's kings to hold one of
its \emph{own} men; a corner king alone has three such squares, so a side needs
four men to be stalemated, and no side here has more than three. Rule 4 never
fires.

\begin{result}[title={Three kings beat two, and it is not close}]{kings}
\textbf{Every white-to-move placement is a win}, on every board computed:
$n = 3$ to $n = 11$, up to $1\,987\,925\,940$ placements at $11\times11$. Not
one draw. With Black to move the endgame is a White win or a draw and nothing else
from $5\times5$ up.

The reason is a single row of the census, and it is the whole endgame:
\emph{up to $14\times14$, three kings against one is won with \textbf{either}
side to move}. So in $K\!+\!K\!+\!K$ vs $K\!+\!K$ \textbf{any capture White can
make wins on the spot}. He takes a king, and it does not matter whether Black
recaptures: without the recapture Black is a lone king against three, and with
it a lone king against two, and both are lost. White has no mating net to build
and no zugzwang to arrange. He has exactly one problem --- get a king beside a
black king on his own move --- and Black has exactly one defence.
\end{result}

\subsection{How the depth grows}\label{sub:kings-growth}

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}
