  width=0.92\textwidth, height=70mm,
  xlabel={board size $n$}, ylabel={deepest win (plies)},
  xmin=3, xmax=17, ymin=0, ymax=130, xtick={3,4,...,17},
  grid=both, grid style={ink!12}, tick align=outside,
  axis line style={ink!45}, label style={color=inksoft}, tick label style={color=inksoft},
  legend style={at={(0.5,-0.24)},anchor=north,draw=ink!20,fill=paper,font=\small,
                legend columns=2, /tikz/every even column/.append style={column sep=6mm}},
  legend cell align=left,
]
\addplot[kings, very thick, mark=*, mark size=1.3] coordinates {
(3,7) (4,13) (5,29) (6,39) (7,49) (8,63) (9,83) (10,101) (11,125)};
\addlegendentry{$K\!+\!K\!+\!K$ vs $K\!+\!K$ --- the endgame}
\addplot[kqkk, thick, mark=square*, mark size=1.1] coordinates {
(3,3) (4,9) (5,17) (6,21) (7,27) (8,33) (9,45) (10,55) (11,69) (12,81) (13,99) (14,123) (15,73) (16,73) (17,73)};
\addlegendentry{$K\!+\!K$ vs $K$ --- the phalanx of \S\ref{sub:cap-hunt}}
\addplot[kings!55, thick, dashed, mark=triangle*, mark size=1.5] coordinates {
(3,3) (4,9) (5,13) (6,17) (7,23) (8,31) (9,37) (10,47) (11,55) (12,65) (13,73) (14,81) (15,91) (16,103) (17,119)};
\addlegendentry{$K\!+\!K\!+\!K$ vs $K$ --- what White converts into}
\addplot[inksoft, thick, dotted, mark=o, mark size=1.1] coordinates {
(3,5) (4,17) (5,19) (6,23) (7,29) (8,35) (9,47) (10,57) (11,71) (12,83) (13,101) (14,125) (15,73) (16,73) (17,73)};
\addlegendentry{$K\!+\!K$ vs $K\!+\!K$ --- what Black converts into}
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\noindent
Four curves, and three things worth saying about them.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{The five-man line runs at roughly twice the three-man line, and
it is climbing faster.} Its steps are $+10$, $+10$, $+14$, $+20$, $+18$, $+24$ from
$5\times5$ to $11\times11$: the step has more than doubled while the board grew
by six.
That is the same untidy climb --- oscillating, but only going one way --- that
$K\!+\!K$ vs $K$ made before it collapsed at $15\times15$
(\S\ref{sub:cap-hunt-length}), which is why \S\ref{sub:kings-break} is careful
about what has and has not been settled.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Two kings against two is exactly two plies worse than two
against one}, at every board from $5\times5$ to $14\times14$: $19/17$, $23/21$,
$29/27$, $35/33$, $47/45$, $57/55$, $71/69$, $83/81$, $101/99$, $125/123$. Ten
board sizes, never off by anything else. The mechanism is visible in the line. At
$8\times8$ the deepest $K\!+\!K$ vs $K\!+\!K$ win is $\text{K}a1$, $\text{K}c1$
against $\text{k}d1$, $\text{k}b3$, and it opens
\textbf{1.\,K}$c1\!\times\!d1$: a black king was simply \emph{hanging}, White
takes it for nothing, and what is left is the deepest $K\!+\!K$ vs $K$ hunt
there is. One ply to take the spare king, one ply for Black's reply, and then
the three-man endgame entire.

And the identity breaks exactly where that mechanism does. At $15\times15$, where
the hunt stops working, both endgames stand at 73 plies and the $+2$ is gone:
past the transition the deepest surviving win is no longer \emph{take the spare
king and then run the longest hunt there is}, but a corner trap that the spare
king adds nothing to. $K\!+\!K$ vs $K\!+\!K$ dies at the same board size as the
endgame it sits on, and far more completely --- from 13.3\,\% of placements won
at $14\times14$ to \textbf{0.03\,\%} at $15\times15$, against
$K\!+\!K$ vs $K$'s fall from 100\,\% to 7.0\,\%. White needs two things to go
right there and Black only one. What survives does not even grow with the
board --- it shrinks: 203\,232 won placements at $15\times15$, 198\,748 at
$16\times16$, 195\,816 at $17\times17$, while $K\!+\!K$ vs $K$'s surviving set
grows like $n^4$ over the same three boards. The wins that are left are a fixed
handful of corner traps, and the board is simply getting bigger around them.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Three kings catch a lone king faster than two do}, from
$5\times5$ on, and the gap widens without bound: $13$ against $17$ at $n=5$,
$31$ against $33$ at $n=8$, $73$ against $99$ at $n=13$, $81$ against $123$ at
$n=14$. A third king is not so much a third pursuer as a third wall, and the
hunt of \S\ref{sub:cap-hunt} is entirely about walls.

\subsubsection{A coincidence, recorded rather than explained}\label{sub:kings-sum}

\noindent
Every depth here is odd, since a white-to-move win ends on White's move, so the
sum of the three-man and the four-man depth is even and cannot equal the
five-man depth exactly. It comes as close as parity allows at every board from
$5\times5$ to $11\times11$:

\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{lrrrrrrr}
\toprule
$n$ & 5 & 6 & 7 & 8 & 9 & 10 & \textbf{11}\\
\midrule
$K\!+\!K$ vs $K$ \ $+$ \ $K\!+\!K\!+\!K$ vs $K$ & $17{+}13$ & $21{+}17$ & $27{+}23$ & $33{+}31$ & $45{+}37$ & $55{+}47$ & $69{+}55$\\
                                                & $=30$     & $=38$     & $=50$     & $=64$     & $=82$     & $=102$    & $=\mathbf{124}$\\
$K\!+\!K\!+\!K$ vs $K\!+\!K$, actual            & 29        & 39        & 49        & 63        & 83        & 101       & $\mathbf{125}$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\noindent
Seven boards, off by one every time. The last column is the only one that was
ever a prediction: the three-man and four-man depths at $11\times11$ were known
long before the five-man table finished, so $124$ was written down here and
$123$ or $125$ was the forecast. It came out $125$. It is still not a law ---
at $4\times4$ it gives $9+9=18$ against a true depth of $13$ --- and the play does not support the obvious reading of it either. If the five-man
win really were \emph{force a capture, then finish}, the first capture in the
deepest line would fall where the two summands meet. It does not:

\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{lrrrrrr}
\toprule
$n$ & 3 & 4 & 5 & 6 & 7 & 8\\
\midrule
deepest win (plies)  & 7 & 13 & 29 & 39 & 49 & 63\\
first capture at ply & 5 & 8  & 17 & 27 & 26 & 43\\
material it leaves   & 3\,v\,1 & 2\,v\,2 & 3\,v\,1 & 3\,v\,1 & 2\,v\,2 & 3\,v\,1\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\noindent
Most of the longest win is spent before a man is taken --- 43 plies of the 63 at
$8\times8$, and never less than half on any board here. Whatever is running away
with the board, it is the \emph{herding} and not the finish. And at $4\times4$ and
$7\times7$ the first capture is \textbf{Black's} --- he takes a white king and
still loses, because the $K\!+\!K$ vs $K\!+\!K$ he reaches is one of the third
that White wins.

\subsection{Whoever moves first wins}\label{sub:kings-tempo}

\noindent
On $3\times3$ and $4\times4$ the extra king is worth nothing at all unless you
also have the move. These are the only two boards on which Black wins anything,
and on both of them the same placements are a White win with White to play and a
\emph{Black} win with Black to play. Three kings against two, and the result is
decided entirely by whose turn it is.

\smallskip

\begin{minipage}{0.47\textwidth}\centering
  \bd{13mm}{3}{%
    \coordsn{3}%
    \pw{0}{0}{\glyphK}\pw{2}{0}{\glyphK}\pw{0}{2}{\glyphK}%
    \pb{1}{0}\pb{2}{2}%
    \arw{0}{0}{1}{0}{arrowW}{1}%
    \arw{2}{2}{1}{2}{arrowB}{2}%
    \arw{0}{2}{1}{2}{arrowW}{3}}\\[1.5mm]
  {\scriptsize\color{inksoft}\textbf{White to move: White wins in 3 plies}\\
   1.\,K$a1\times b1$ \ k$c3-b3$ \ 2.\,K$a3\times b3$\#}
\end{minipage}\hfill
\begin{minipage}{0.47\textwidth}\centering
  \bd{13mm}{3}{%
    \coordsn{3}%
    \pw{0}{0}{\glyphK}\pw{2}{0}{\glyphK}\pw{0}{2}{\glyphK}%
    \pb{1}{0}\pb{2}{2}%
    \arw{1}{0}{0}{0}{arrowB}{1}%
    \arw{2}{0}{2}{1}{arrowW}{2}%
    \arw{2}{2}{2}{1}{arrowB}{3}%
    \arw{0}{2}{1}{2}{arrowW}{4}%
    \arw{2}{1}{1}{2}{arrowB}{5}}\\[1.5mm]
  {\scriptsize\color{inksoft}\textbf{Black to move: BLACK wins in 5 plies}\\
   1\ldots\,k$b1\times a1$ \ 2.\,K$c1-c2$ \ k$c3\times c2$\\
   3.\,K$a3-b3$ \ k$c2\times b3$\#}
\end{minipage}

\smallskip

\noindent
The same five men, the same nine squares, and the move is the whole game. With
White to play the black king on $b1$ is standing beside two white kings and is
taken at once; with Black to play it takes first, and White's remaining two
kings cannot both be defended on a board this small. Twelve placements on
$3\times3$ behave this way and \textbf{404} on $4\times4$, where the margin is
wider still --- White wins in 9 plies with the move, Black in 17 without it,
which is the deepest Black win anywhere in this endgame:

\begin{center}
  \bd{11mm}{4}{%
    \coordsn{4}%
    \pw{0}{0}{\glyphK}\pw{3}{0}{\glyphK}\pw{0}{2}{\glyphK}%
    \pb{1}{0}\pb{3}{2}}\\[1.5mm]
  {\scriptsize\color{inksoft}$4\times4$: K$a1$ K$d1$ K$a3$ vs k$b1$ k$d3$ ---
   White to move wins in 9, Black to move wins in 17}
\end{center}

\noindent
From $5\times5$ up this stops: Black never wins again on any board computed, and
the most the move is worth to him is a draw. But the move is never worth
\emph{less} than that to either side, which is the other half of the same
observation --- \textbf{this endgame contains no zugzwang at all}. A white-to-move
placement is always a win and a black-to-move placement is always a win or a
draw, so neither side is ever worse off for having to move. \S\ref{sub:kn-claim}
says the same of two knights, for an entirely different reason.

\subsection{Black's one resource, and the king he must take}\label{sub:kings-grab}

\noindent
Since every quiet black move hands White a white-to-move placement, and those
are all won, Black's position is drawn \emph{precisely} when he has a capture
that leads to a drawn $K\!+\!K$ vs $K\!+\!K$, and for no other reason. That is
the entire defence. It is also very sharp. Here is a $6\times6$ placement with
Black to move and ten legal moves, of which \textbf{three} take a white king ---
and only one of the three draws.

\smallskip

\begin{minipage}{0.47\textwidth}\centering
  \bd{9mm}{6}{%
    \coordsn{6}%
    \pw{0}{0}{\glyphK}\pw{1}{0}{\glyphK}\pw{2}{0}{\glyphK}%
    \pb{3}{0}\pb{0}{1}%
    \arw{0}{1}{0}{0}{arrowB}{1}%
    \arw{1}{0}{0}{0}{arrowW}{2}%
    \arw{3}{0}{2}{0}{arrowB}{3}}\\[1.5mm]
  {\scriptsize\color{inksoft}\textbf{1\ldots\,k$a2\times a1$ draws}\\
   2.\,K$b1\times a1$ \ k$d1\times c1$ --- and the bare kings\\
   stand on $a1$ and $c1$, two apart}
\end{minipage}\hfill
\begin{minipage}{0.47\textwidth}\centering
  \bd{9mm}{6}{%
    \coordsn{6}%
    \pw{0}{0}{\glyphK}\pw{1}{0}{\glyphK}\pw{2}{0}{\glyphK}%
    \pb{3}{0}\pb{0}{1}%
    \arw{0}{1}{1}{0}{arrowB}{1}%
    \arw{0}{0}{1}{0}{arrowW}{2}%
    \arw{3}{0}{2}{0}{arrowB}{3}%
    \arw{1}{0}{2}{0}{arrowW}{4}}\\[1.5mm]
  {\scriptsize\color{inksoft}\textbf{1\ldots\,k$a2\times b1$ loses in 19}\\
   2.\,K$a1\times b1$ \ k$d1\times c1$ \ 3.\,K$b1\times c1$ ---\\
   they stand on $b1$ and $c1$, and touch}
\end{minipage}

\smallskip

\noindent
One square's difference in which king Black takes, and the same sequence of
trades runs off the board either into two kings that touch or two that do not.
The smallest endgame of all decides it: bare $K$ vs $K$ is drawn, neither king
being able to force anything, \emph{unless} the two already stand beside each
other --- in which case whoever is to move simply takes.
\S\ref{sub:cap-changes} is that same observation with a queen still on the board.
Black's third capture, $1\ldots$\,k$d1\times c1$, loses the same way and in the
same 19 plies. The king it is right to take is the one buried in the corner,
which is the one that looks least worth having.

At $9\times9$ this defence saves Black in $86\,427\,016$ of $256\,215\,960$
black-to-move placements, a third of them; the share falls steadily with the
board --- 51.2\%, 47.0\%, 42.6\%, 38.1\%, 33.7\%, 29.8\% from $5\times5$ to
$10\times10$ --- for the dull reason that men scattered at random on a bigger
board are less often beside one another.

\subsection{White gives a king back, and that is how he wins}\label{sub:kings-trade}

\noindent
The other side of the same coin. Because any capture wins on the spot, an even
exchange is not even: it takes $3$ vs $2$ down to $2$ vs $1$, and $2$ vs $1$ is
won. So in the longest win on the board White walks into the trade on purpose.
Below is the $6\times6$ deepest win --- K$a1$ K$f1$ K$a6$ against k$d2$ k$a4$, 39
plies --- caught at ply 27, after thirteen moves of herding and with 13 plies
left:

\smallskip

\begin{minipage}{0.47\textwidth}\centering
  \bd{9mm}{6}{%
    \coordsn{6}%
    \pw{3}{2}{\glyphK}\pw{3}{3}{\glyphK}\pw{1}{5}{\glyphK}%
    \pb{4}{5}\pb{3}{4}%
    \arw{3}{3}{3}{4}{arrowW}{1}%
    \arw{4}{5}{3}{4}{arrowB}{2}}\\[1.5mm]
  {\scriptsize\color{inksoft}\textbf{White to move, wins in 13}\\
   14.\,K$d4\times d5$ \ k$e6\times d5$ --- a king for a king}
\end{minipage}\hfill
\begin{minipage}{0.47\textwidth}\centering
  \bd{9mm}{6}{%
    \coordsn{6}%
    \pw{3}{2}{\glyphK}\pw{1}{5}{\glyphK}%
    \pb{3}{4}}\\[1.5mm]
  {\scriptsize\color{inksoft}\textbf{$K\!+\!K$ vs $K$, and White is winning}\\
   15.\,K$b6-b5$ \ k$d5-e5$ \ 16.\,K$b5-c5$ \ k$e5-f6$\\
   17.\,K$d3-e4$ \ k$f6-e6$ \ 18.\,K$e4-f4$ \ k$e6-f6$\\
   19.\,K$c5-d5$ \ k$f6-e6$ \ 20.\,K$d5\times e6$\#}
\end{minipage}

\smallskip

\noindent
Two plies, no change in material, and the endgame is over: three against two has
become two against one, which \S\ref{sub:cap-hunt} showed is won from every
placement there is. It is the exact converse of \S\ref{sub:kings-grab} ---
Black's trades are his only salvation and White's his fastest route home ---
and both come from the same place. With kings only, what a trade changes is not
the \emph{balance} but which endgame you are in.

\subsection{The hardest position is White's own men in his own way}\label{sub:kings-hardest}

\noindent
\S\ref{sub:kn-hardest} finds that the deepest KNNNK win is not a black king in
the safest corner but White's three knights tangled among themselves. Three
kings do the same thing, and more plainly. At every board from $8\times8$ to
$17\times17$ the deepest $K\!+\!K\!+\!K$ vs $K$ placement is the same shape, and
at most sizes the same squares: \textbf{K$a1$ K$b1$ K$c1$} against a black king
a couple of files away. It takes 47 plies at $10\times10$.

\begin{center}
  \bd{9mm}{10}{%
    \coordsn{10}%
    \ring{9}{9}{hlmark}%
    \pw{0}{0}{\glyphK}\pw{1}{0}{\glyphK}\pw{2}{0}{\glyphK}%
    \pb{4}{0}%
    \arw{0}{0}{1}{1}{arrowW}{1}%
    \arw{4}{0}{5}{0}{arrowB}{2}%
    \arw{1}{0}{2}{1}{arrowW}{3}%
    \arw{5}{0}{6}{0}{arrowB}{4}%
    \arw{1}{1}{2}{2}{arrowW}{5}%
    \arw{6}{0}{7}{1}{arrowB}{6}}\\[1.5mm]
  {\scriptsize\color{inksoft}$10\times10$, White to move, wins in 47 plies.
   1.\,K$a1-b2$ k$e1-f1$ \ 2.\,K$b1-c2$ k$f1-g1$ \ 3.\,K$b2-c3$ k$g1-h2$
   \ $\ldots$ \ 24.\,K$i9\times j10$\#\\
   The black king runs the length of the board and is taken in the ringed
   corner, 71 squares from where he started.}
\end{center}

\noindent
White's difficulty is not finding the black king; it is that his own three
kings begin jammed on three squares of one edge, where each blocks the others
and only one of them can move usefully at a time. The first six moves are
entirely deployment. That the deepest position of a completely won endgame
should be one in which the winning side is in its own way is a small joke the
tables keep making, and it is worth noticing that it survives when every man on
the board is identical: it is not about knights.

\subsection{Where it must break, and why that is out of reach}\label{sub:kings-break}

\noindent
Everything above rests on one clause --- \emph{any capture White can make wins
on the spot} --- and that clause is a statement about $n \le 14$. It expires:

\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
 & $13$ & $\mathbf{14}$ & $\mathbf{15}$ & $16$ & $17$\\
\midrule
$K\!+\!K$ vs $K$, white to move        & all won & \textbf{all won} & \textbf{7.0\% won} & 6.0\% & 5.3\%\\
$K\!+\!K\!+\!K$ vs $K$, white to move  & all won & all won & \textbf{all won} & all won & all won\\
$K\!+\!K\!+\!K$ vs $K$, black to move  & all won & all won & \textbf{8.9\% drawn} & 8.0\% & 7.2\%\\
$K\!+\!K$ vs $K\!+\!K$, white to move   & 15.1\% won & \textbf{13.3\% won} & \textbf{0.03\% won} & 0.02\% & 0.01\%\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\noindent
Two kings stop beating one at $15\times15$, which is \S\ref{sub:cap-hunt-length}'s
transition seen from the other colour. \textbf{Three kings still beat one} ---
all $415\,850\,400$ white-to-move placements at $15\times15$ are won, and so are
$16\times16$'s and $17\times17$'s. But the black-to-move half goes, and every one
of the new draws is the same draw: Black takes a white king and reaches the
$K\!+\!K$ vs $K$ that has just stopped being won. That is precisely the
recapture the five-man win was assuming could be ignored.

Black's side of it gets easier at the same moment, and by more. His entire
defence is a capture into a \emph{drawn} $K\!+\!K$ vs $K\!+\!K$
(\S\ref{sub:kings-grab}), and that endgame collapses at $15\times15$ along with
the one underneath it. The share of it that is drawn with White to move runs
84.9\,\% at $13\times13$, 86.7\,\% at $14\times14$, and \textbf{99.97\,\%} at
$15\times15$. Up to $14\times14$ Black has to choose his capture ---
\S\ref{sub:kings-grab} shows a placement offering three of them where only one
saves. Past the transition he barely has to look: almost any king he can take
will do.

It costs White time in the half he still wins, too. The
$K\!+\!K\!+\!K$ vs $K$ depth runs 31, 37, 47, 55, 65, 73, 81, 91, 103 and
then 119, for $n = 8 \ldots 17$: steps of $+6$ to $+10$ a board all the way to
$15\times15$,
and then $+12$ and $+16$. The turn begins exactly where the endgame underneath
it broke, which is what one would expect if these transitions propagate up the
lattice rather than arising independently at each level.

\begin{result}[title={What is settled here, and what is not}]{kings}
$K\!+\!K\!+\!K$ vs $K\!+\!K$ is a White win from every white-to-move placement
for $3 \le n \le 11$. That is a computation, not an argument, and it does not
extend past $14\times14$ by the reasoning given above, because the reasoning
stops being true there.

Whether the endgame survives its own transition is \textbf{not settled}. The
depth climbing at $+18$ a board is a symptom and not a proof in either
direction. What would settle it is out of reach by two board sizes: the
$15\times15$ table is $5.9\times10^{9}$ canonical slots per side to move,
24\,GB at two bytes an entry, against the 2.4\,GB that $12\times12$ costs.
\end{result}

\section{Three knights, and the mate that two of them cannot force}\label{sec:knights}

Every table in \S\S\ref{sec:endgames}--5 rests on one sentence, stated as an invariant in
\texttt{geometry.hpp}: whatever Black captures, what is left cannot mate, so
the capture ends the game in a draw and lies outside the table. \S\ref{sec:kqkr} broke that
sentence by arming Black. This section breaks it from the other side. Black
stays bare to the last move --- he never has anything but a king --- and it is
\emph{White's} remaining material that stops being harmless.

\subsection{What ``two knights cannot mate'' actually says}\label{sub:kn-claim}

The textbook fact is that two knights cannot \emph{force} mate. It is easy to
hear that as ``two knights cannot mate'', and the difference is the whole
section: the two readings put this endgame on opposite sides of the invariant.

One knight is the strong reading, and it is exactly true. Building K+N vs K on
every board from $3\times3$ to $24\times24$ and counting mates gives zero every
time --- not no \emph{forced} mate, but no placement of the three men in which
Black is mated at all. The endgame is dead: its only other terminal is
stalemate, and there are \textbf{exactly 40 of those on every board from
$4\times4$ up} --- every one of them with the black king in a \emph{corner},
ten arrangements per corner, the same forty however much board is added around
them.

Two knights are the weak reading, and the mates are real.

\begin{result}[title={K + N + N vs K: mates exist; zugzwang does not}]{knnk}
On $8\times8$ there are \textbf{120} placements in which a lone black king is
mated by king and two knights. The count is exactly $16n-8$ on every board from
$4\times4$ up.

\smallskip
And yet the deepest white win is \textbf{one ply}, on every board size from
$3\times3$ to $16\times16$: \textbf{White wins only where he mates on the
move.} The number of such placements is exactly $104n-216$ for $n \ge 5$ ---
out of $1.9\times10^9$ legal ones at $16\times16$, which is one in a million
and a half.

\smallskip
That is the machine-checkable form of the textbook sentence. It is not that
mates do not exist; it is that \textbf{Black is never in zugzwang}, so he is
never obliged to walk into one. Black-to-move losses and mates are the same
number on every board --- a position is lost only when it is already over.

\smallskip
\textbf{With one exception, and it is the smallest board.} On $3\times3$ there
are 36 black-to-move losses against 32 mates: one symmetry class, four
placements, in which Black has moves, all of them step into mate, and the
squeeze that cannot happen on a real board happens because there is nowhere
else to stand.
\end{result}

\subsection{The same two knights, playing for the king itself}\label{sub:kn-capture}

\S\ref{sec:capture} replaced mate by the capture it stands for. Doing the same thing here
costs one clause and changes the endgame completely.

For this material the capture rules reduce to something very simple. The
theorem of \S\ref{sub:cap-onceking} says they are chess with two clauses replaced --- stalemate
becomes a loss, and a \emph{checkmate} becomes a draw when the mated side is
totally immobile under rule 2. The second clause cannot fire here: a king may
step onto attacked squares, so it is blocked only by its own men and the edge
of the board, a cornered king has three on-board neighbours, and two knights can
block at most two of them. Neither side can ever be totally immobile. So for
K\,+\,N\,+\,N vs K, \textbf{the capture rules are exactly chess with stalemate
scored as a loss}, and nothing else changes at all.

That single clause is worth the whole endgame, because two knights cannot force
mate but they \emph{can} force stalemate.

\begin{result}[title={One clause, and a drawn endgame becomes a won one}]{knnk}
\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrrrr@{}}
\toprule
$n$ & 4 & 6 & 8 & 12 & \textbf{14} & \textbf{15} & 16 \\
\midrule
chess: White wins        & 120 & 408 & 616 & 1\,032 & 1\,240 & 1\,344 & 1\,448 \\
\quad share              & 1.28\,\% & 0.09\,\% & 0.011\,\% & 0.0006\,\% & 0.0002\,\% & 0.0001\,\% & 0.00008\,\% \\
\addlinespace
capture: White wins      & 9\,348 & 447\,832 & 5\,713\,976 & 179\,381\,448 & 644\,455\,884 & 5\,162\,568 & 5\,461\,920 \\
\quad share              & \textbf{100\,\%} & \textbf{100\,\%} & 99.38\,\% & 99.85\,\% & \textbf{99.92\,\%} & \textbf{0.45\,\%} & 0.28\,\% \\
\quad deepest (plies)    & 15 & 57 & 51 & 111 & \textbf{219} & 97 & 95 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\smallskip
On $8\times8$ the same men on the same board go from \textbf{616} won
placements to \textbf{5\,713\,976} --- a factor of 9\,276 --- and up to
$6\times6$ White wins \emph{every} legal position.

\smallskip
Two figures say the rule is doing exactly what it claims and nothing more. The
\textbf{mate count is identical under both rules} --- $16n-8$ either way, so no
mate is created or destroyed. And the \textbf{chess stalemate count equals the
capture-rule loss count exactly}: 3\,864 at $8\times8$, both. The clause
relabels precisely that set.
\end{result}

\noindent
This is what the relabelled position looks like. Black is not in check, has no
move, and every square he would like is covered --- $a2$ is defended by the
king, so it cannot be taken:

\begin{center}
\begin{minipage}{0.62\textwidth}\centering
\bdw{2.6cm}{8}{\pw{0}{0}{\glyphK}\pw{0}{1}{\glyphN}\pw{2}{4}{\glyphN}\pb{0}{2}}\\[1.5mm]
{\small\color{knnk}wK\,$a1$, wN\,$a2$, wN\,$c5$, bK\,$a3$ ---
\textbf{a draw in chess, a loss under capture rules}}
\end{minipage}
\end{center}

\subsection{And then the win disappears again}\label{sub:kn-vanish}

The capture-rule win does not last. It has a critical board size, and the
signature is the one \S\ref{sec:kqkr} and \S\ref{sub:cap-hunt} both found: the depth runs away, and then
the win goes.

\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrrrrr@{}}
\toprule
$n$ & 8 & 9 & 10 & 11 & 12 & 13 & \textbf{14} & \textbf{15} \\
\midrule
deepest win (plies) & 51 & 61 & 75 & 91 & 111 & 151 & \textbf{219} & 97 \\
White's share       & 99.38 & 99.57 & 99.70 & 99.79 & 99.85 & 99.89 & \textbf{99.92\,\%} & \textbf{0.45\,\%} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\noindent
Both transitions on one axis, with the ordinary rule for comparison. The
vertical scale is logarithmic because the two rules are six orders of magnitude
apart: same colour means same material, and the line style is the rule.

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[
  width=0.91\textwidth, height=62mm,
  xlabel={board size $n$}, ylabel={White's share of legal placements},
  xmin=3, xmax=16, xtick={3,4,...,16},
  ymode=log, ymin=4e-5, ymax=400,
  ytick={0.0001,0.001,0.01,0.1,1,10,100},
