\noindent
Three knights under the ordinary rules and two knights under the capture rules
sit almost on top of each other up to $14\times14$ --- the extra knight and the
changed clause are worth about the same amount --- and then the capture line
falls off a cliff the KNNNK line never reaches. The lone knight makes the same
fall nine boards earlier, and what is left of it afterwards is not a share at
all but a fixed number of positions, which is why its line decays as a straight
$n^{-4}$ slope: 2\,900 wins against $n^6$ placements.

\noindent
One placement makes it concrete, the same four men on the same four squares
throughout --- the white king in the corner with both knights near it and the
black king five files away:

\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrrrr@{}}
\toprule
$n$ & 8 & 10 & 11 & 12 & 13 & \textbf{14} & \textbf{15} \\
\midrule
wK\,$a1$, wN\,$c2$, wN\,$c3$, bK\,$e5$ & mate in 20 & 32 & 41 & 50 & 70 &
\textbf{103} & \textbf{draw} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\noindent
Twenty moves, then 32, 41, 50, 70, 103 --- and then nothing. It is the KQKR
fortress of \S\ref{sub:kqkr-oneposition} in different material: a win that gets longer and longer as
the board grows until, at one particular size, the room Black gains is enough
and it stops being a win at all. Two knights need to walk a king into a corner
to stalemate it, and past $14\times14$ he can go round them.

\smallskip
\noindent
\noindent
And here is that transition on a single quiet placement --- \emph{quiet} in the
sense \S\ref{sub:kqkr-quiet} uses for the KQKR draws: nothing whatever is attacked. The black
king is not in check, neither knight stands on a square he attacks, and the
kings are two files apart. The two boards carry the same four men on the same
four squares; the only difference is one extra rank and file. The ring marks
$d2$, Black's only move --- \textcolor{arrowW}{green} where White can still
cope with it, \textcolor{arrowB}{red} where it saves him.

\begin{center}
\begin{minipage}{0.49\textwidth}\centering
\bd{0.34cm}{14}{\pw{0}{0}{\glyphK}\pw{4}{0}{\glyphN}\pw{2}{2}{\glyphN}\pb{2}{0}%
\ring{3}{1}{arrowW}}\\[2mm]
{\small\color{knnk}$14\times14$ --- white to move,\\\textbf{White wins, 104 moves}}
\end{minipage}\hfill
\begin{minipage}{0.49\textwidth}\centering
\bd{0.34cm}{15}{\pw{0}{0}{\glyphK}\pw{4}{0}{\glyphN}\pw{2}{2}{\glyphN}\pb{2}{0}%
\ring{3}{1}{arrowB}}\\[2mm]
{\small\color{knnk}$15\times15$ --- white to move,\\\textbf{drawn}}
\end{minipage}
\end{center}

\noindent
wK\,$a1$, wN\,$e1$, wN\,$c3$, bK\,$c1$. What makes it worth a diagram is how
close White is to winning on the spot. The black king has five squares on the
board and \textbf{four of them are covered} --- $b1$ and $d1$ by the knight on
$c3$, $b2$ by the white king, $c2$ by the knight on $e1$ --- and under these
rules stepping onto a covered square is legal and loses at once. Only the
ringed square, $d2$, is free. So Black has exactly one move, White is one
tempo away from the stalemate that would win him the game, and nothing is
hanging anywhere.

\smallskip
\noindent
On $14\times14$ that is enough: Black runs out through $d2$ and White spends
104 moves putting him back. On $15\times15$, with one more rank and one more
file to run into, it is not enough at all --- and this one placement carries
the whole runaway on its own: \textbf{mate in 52 at $12\times12$, 71 at
$13\times13$, 104 at $14\times14$, and then drawn at $15$ and at $16$.}
\textbf{The draw is not bought by a threat, a hanging knight or a tempo --- it
is bought by the extra rank.} Every value comes from \texttt{./kqk probe}, and
the whole $15\times15$ table holds 141\,702\,005 drawn white-to-move classes
against 645\,603 won ones.

\begin{center}\small
\textbf{K\,+\,N vs K under capture rules}\\[1mm]
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrrrrrr@{}}
\toprule
$n$ & 4 & 5 & \textbf{6} & \textbf{7} & 8 & 10 & 12 & 14 & 16 \\
\midrule
White wins & 1\,764 & 8\,792 & \textbf{30\,824} &
  \textbf{3\,180} & 2\,844 & 2\,900 & 2\,900 & 2\,900 & 2\,900 \\
share & 98.9\,\% & 98.9\,\% & \textbf{99.5\,\%} & \textbf{3.7\,\%} &
  1.4\,\% & 0.34\,\% & 0.11\,\% & 0.04\,\% & 0.02\,\% \\
deepest (plies) & 23 & 33 & \textbf{65} & \textbf{29} & 27 & 27 & 27 & 27 & 27 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\noindent
\textbf{The knight below does the same thing, sooner.} K\,+\,N vs K under the
same rules is a win from $99.5\,\%$ of $6\times6$ placements --- a lone knight
and king really can force a bare king into stalemate on a small enough board
--- and then collapses at $n=7$ to a fixed handful: exactly \textbf{2\,900
placements on every board from $10\times10$ up}, never deeper than 27 plies,
and \textbf{exactly 40 stalemate positions} on every board from $4\times4$ up
whatever the size. That is why the KNNK capture table converts into a KNK
capture table rather than scoring $\ldots$K$\times$N as a draw: it is not one.

\begin{result}[title={How this one was checked, and what it caught}]{knnk}
The unreduced solver disagreed with the table, and it was right. Phase B's
two-phase alternation assumes a black-to-move loss always has value $d+2$; a
\emph{conversion} can name any depth at all, and KNK under capture rules
reaches 65 plies, so values were being written that the alternation never came
back to retract from. KNNNK had escaped this only because the KNNK table
beneath it is one ply deep. The fix is the bucketed induction \S\ref{sec:capture} needed for
the same reason, in the smallest form a one-way conversion allows, and it is
allocated only for a table that actually converts.

\smallskip
What stands behind the numbers above: Bellman re-derivation for
$10 \le n \le 16$, \emph{including} both sides of the transition; the unreduced
solver over every ordered placement for $n \le 10$; and a third solver written
from the rules of \S\ref{sec:capture} rather than from the theorem --- kings capturable, free to
walk into attack, the game ending on the king capture --- which reproduces the
table exactly at $n=4$ and $n=5$ ($9\,348$ and $82\,628$ wins, no draws) with
every depth larger by \textbf{exactly two plies}, which is the theorem being
right: the capture game really plays out Black's forced step and White's
capture where chess-with-stalemate-as-a-loss simply scores the stalemate.
\end{result}

\subsection{What that costs the table above it}\label{sub:kn-cost}

Three knights beat a lone king. But when the black king takes one of them the
position left is K+N+N vs K, and the previous page says that is sometimes a
white win. So KNNNK cannot score $\ldots$K$\times$N as a draw the way KQK,
KRK, KBBK and KBNK all score their captures. It has to look the answer up.

That makes it the second endgame here that converts, after KQKR, and the first
whose conversion runs one way only: Black can capture and White never can, and
the table it converts into holds wins and draws but no losses, so the entries
stay unsigned bytes. The chain is three deep and the bottom two are free:

\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}llll@{}}
\toprule
& \textbf{Black's capture leaves} & \textbf{which is} & \textbf{so} \\
\midrule
KNK   & bare kings & drawn, nothing can mate & no table needed \\
KNNK  & KNK        & drawn, \emph{no mate exists} & no table needed \\
KNNNK & KNNK       & \textbf{sometimes won} & a table is needed \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\noindent
The cost of that table is nothing: KNNK at $16\times16$ is 520\,MB, seconds to
build, against the 41\,GiB the KNNNK table above it would need. What it buys is
the difference between a computed answer and an assumed one --- and the
assumption is false, so it buys correctness rather than tidiness.

\smallskip
\noindent
The index needs one new thing, and it is the natural extension of the one KBBK
uses: three like knights are an \emph{unordered triple}, so a configuration is
one of $\binom{n^2}{3}$ rather than $n^6$, and the table is six times smaller
than the ordered encoding for the same reason two like bishops halve it.

\subsection{Three knights win, and where they do not}\label{sub:kn-win}

\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}rrrrrr@{}}
\toprule
$n$ & \textbf{entries / side} & \textbf{white to move: won} &
\textbf{drawn} & \textbf{drawn \%} & \textbf{deepest} \\
\midrule
 4 &          11\,760 &            26\,684 &        3\,004 & 10.119\,\% & 10 \\
 5 &        144\,900 &          464\,060 &      23\,216 &  4.764\,\% & 10 \\
 6 &        963\,900 &      4\,058\,156 &    121\,372 &  2.904\,\% & 16 \\
 7 &     4\,974\,480 &     23\,605\,308 &    454\,992 &  1.891\,\% & 18 \\
 8 &    19\,248\,768 &    104\,116\,284 & 1\,342\,972 &  1.273\,\% & 21 \\
 9 &    65\,696\,400 &    375\,731\,528 & 3\,325\,180 &  0.877\,\% & 25 \\
10 &   189\,189\,000 & 1\,164\,446\,876 & 7\,223\,020 &  0.616\,\% & 29 \\
11 &   505\,404\,900 & 3\,202\,419\,096 & 14\,233\,372 & 0.442\,\% & 33 \\
12 & 1\,206\,176\,400 & 7\,998\,968\,276 & 26\,013\,604 & 0.324\,\% & 36 \\
13 & 2\,738\,195\,460 & 18\,457\,732\,040 & 44\,791\,156 & 0.242\,\% & 41 \\
14 & 5\,735\,254\,980 & 39\,855\,641\,724 & 73\,475\,404 & 0.184\,\% & 45 \\
15 & 11\,591\,361\,600 & 81\,335\,868\,656 & 115\,781\,260 & 0.142\,\% & 50 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\noindent
Two readings, and the first of them is a question this range cannot settle.
The depth grows by \textbf{about four moves for every unit of board size} ---
against the queen's one and a half --- but \S\ref{sub:kbnk-law} argues that a knight-only
endgame ought to grow \emph{quadratically}, because a knight needs $\Theta(n)$
moves to cross the board and the procedure needs $\Theta(n)$ repositionings.
Three knights are as knight-only as an endgame gets, so that argument applies
here with nothing to soften it. Does the measurement show it?

\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrrrrr@{}}
\toprule
boards compared & $6{\to}8$ & $7{\to}9$ & $8{\to}10$ & $9{\to}11$ & $10{\to}12$ & $11{\to}13$ & $12{\to}14$ & $13{\to}15$ \\
\midrule
KNNNK, moves per unit $n$ & 2.50 & 3.50 & 4.00 & 4.00 & 3.50 & 4.00 & \textbf{4.50} & \textbf{4.50} \\
KNNNK, local exponent $\alpha$ & 0.95 & 1.31 & 1.45 & 1.38 & 1.19 & 1.30 & 1.45 & 1.39 \\
\addlinespace
KQK, local exponent $\alpha$ & 1.24 & 1.27 & 0.82 & 1.20 & 1.22 & 1.16 & 1.18 & 1.14 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\noindent
The same four endgames by \emph{depth}, with KQK underneath as the control,
since its law is exactly linear and its line is therefore what a straight one
looks like on this axis:

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[
  width=0.91\textwidth, height=58mm,
  xlabel={board size $n$}, ylabel={deepest win (moves)},
  xmin=3, xmax=16, ymin=0, ymax=120, xtick={3,4,...,16},
  grid=both, grid style={ink!12}, tick align=outside,
  axis line style={ink!45}, label style={color=inksoft}, tick label style={color=inksoft},
  legend style={at={(0.5,-0.30)},anchor=north,draw=ink!20,fill=paper,font=\footnotesize,
                legend columns=2, /tikz/every even column/.append style={column sep=4mm}},
  legend cell align=left,
]
\addplot[knnk, very thick, mark=*, mark size=1.2] coordinates {
(3,4) (4,8) (5,13) (6,29) (7,22) (8,26) (9,31) (10,38) (11,46) (12,56) (13,76)
(14,110) (15,49) (16,48)};
\addlegendentry{KNNK, \textbf{capture} --- 110 moves at $n=14$, then 49}
\addplot[knnnk, thick, mark=diamond*, mark size=1.6] coordinates {
(3,1) (4,10) (5,10) (6,16) (7,18) (8,21) (9,25) (10,29) (11,33) (12,36) (13,41) (14,45)};
\addlegendentry{KNNNK, chess --- linear, or bending?}
\addplot[ink!55, thick, mark=triangle*, mark size=1.5] coordinates {
(3,4) (4,12) (5,17) (6,33) (7,15) (8,14) (9,14) (10,14) (11,14) (12,14) (13,14)
(14,14) (15,14) (16,14)};
\addlegendentry{KNK, capture --- 33 moves at $n=6$, then 14 for ever}
\addplot[kqk, thick, dashed, mark=square*, mark size=1.0] coordinates {
(3,1) (4,4) (5,6) (6,7) (7,8) (8,10) (9,11) (10,12) (11,14) (12,15) (13,17)
(14,18) (15,20) (16,21)};
\addlegendentry{KQK --- the control: a law that \emph{is} linear}
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\noindent
Two of these four lines end in a cliff, and the shape before the cliff is the
same both times: a depth that climbs faster and faster right up to the board
where the win disappears. The KNK line does it at $n=6$ and the KNNK capture
line at $n=14$, eight boards apart, with one knight between them. Against
those, KQK is almost boringly straight --- and KNNNK, the line this subsection
is about, is the one that cannot yet be told apart from it.

\noindent
\textbf{Still not decided, but the slope has moved.} It climbs from $2.5$ to
$4$, sits at $4$ for three comparisons, and then reads $4.5$ for the last
\emph{two} --- one reading at the end of a range is a wobble, two in a row is
the beginning of a trend, though it is still only two. The exponent does not
separate from the control: KQK's depth is \emph{exactly} linear, and across
these eight comparisons its measured exponent wanders from $0.82$ to $1.27$
while KNNNK's wanders from $0.95$ to $1.45$. The ranges overlap over most of
their length. Eight board sizes are not enough to tell a linear law from the
quadratic one \S\ref{sub:kbnk-law} predicts --- KBNK needed the range $9$ to $21$ before its
early and late slopes ($5.25$ against $7.00$) parted company convincingly.

\smallskip
\noindent
So: \textbf{about four moves per unit of board size over $8 \le n \le 15$,
$4.5$ over the last two comparisons, and no claim about what it does after
that.} The cost of one more term is the reason it stops here rather than
anywhere in particular: $15\times15$ is $21.6$\,GiB and took four and a
quarter hours built against a file, and every board after it doubles.

\smallskip
\noindent
And the win is \emph{not} universal, which no other bare-king endgame here can
say. One placement in eighty is drawn at $8\times8$. The share falls steadily
with the board --- roughly like $n^{-3}$ --- because it measures how often
Black's king starts within reach of a knight, and a bigger board puts him
further away. There is no sign of the reversal that \S\ref{sec:kqkr} and \S\ref{sub:cap-hunt} both found;
up to $13\times13$ this curve only goes down.

\subsection{What escapes, and it is what you would guess}\label{sub:kn-escape}

The obvious hypothesis is that the draws are the positions where a knight
hangs. In \S\ref{sec:kqkr} the obvious hypothesis about KQKR's fortresses turned out to be
wrong when it was measured. This one is right, and measuring it is what says
so. Sorting the white-to-move placements by what the black king is attacking:

\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrrr@{}}
\toprule
& \multicolumn{3}{c}{\textbf{drawn}} & \multicolumn{3}{c}{\textbf{won}} \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-4}\cmidrule(lr){5-7}
$n$ & loose & guarded & quiet & loose & guarded & quiet \\
\midrule
 8 & \textbf{94.9\,\%} & 0.0\,\% &  5.1\,\% & 23.6\,\% & 5.8\,\% & 70.6\,\% \\
10 & \textbf{90.6\,\%} & 0.0\,\% &  9.4\,\% & 17.4\,\% & 2.7\,\% & 80.0\,\% \\
12 & \textbf{88.5\,\%} & 0.0\,\% & 11.5\,\% & 13.1\,\% & 1.4\,\% & 85.6\,\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\noindent
\emph{Loose} means some knight stands on a square the black king attacks and
nothing defends it; \emph{guarded} means one is attacked but defended, so the
threat is empty; \emph{quiet} means the black king attacks nothing at all. The
contrast is the point: a hanging knight is present in nine drawn positions in
ten and in one won position in eight, and the gap widens with the board.

\smallskip
\noindent
Two smaller things fall out of the same census. The \textbf{guarded} column is
not merely small, it is \emph{constant}: exactly \textbf{128 placements on
every board from $7\times7$ to $12\times12$}, a fixed handful that does not
grow with the board at all --- the same shape as the fixed corner cages \S\ref{sec:kqkk}
found under the loose mating rule. And the \textbf{quiet} draws, the ones with
nothing whatever hanging, are a share that climbs the whole way: $0.1\,\%$ of
the draws at $n=5$, $5.1\,\%$ at $n=8$, $11.5\,\%$ at $n=12$, with the
increments shrinking each time. Those are the positions where Black is not
winning a knight now but cannot be stopped from winning one later, and they are
the part of this endgame a bigger board makes relatively more common while
making draws absolutely rarer.

\noindent
Here is one of them on the largest board built, $15\times15$, and it needs no
commentary beyond what is on the squares. Nothing whatever is attacked: the
black king is not in check from any of the three knights, no knight stands on a
square he attacks, and the kings are three squares apart. Every white man is
within three squares of the black king, so the draw is not a matter of White's
army being somewhere else.

\begin{center}
\begin{minipage}{0.44\textwidth}\centering
\bd{0.32cm}{15}{\pw{5}{4}{\glyphK}\pw{0}{0}{\glyphN}\pw{4}{4}{\glyphN}%
\pw{5}{5}{\glyphN}\pb{2}{2}\ring{1}{2}{arrowB}\ring{2}{1}{arrowB}}
\end{minipage}\hfill
\begin{minipage}{0.52\textwidth}
{\small
wK\,$f5$, wN\,$a1$, wN\,$e5$, wN\,$f6$, bK\,$c3$ --- white to move,
\textbf{\color{knnnk}drawn}.

\smallskip
\textbf{The $a1$ knight is trapped.} It has exactly two moves, $b3$ and $c2$,
both ringed --- and both are attacked by the black king and defended by
nothing: the knights on $e5$ and $f6$ reach neither square, and the white king
is three squares from one and four from the other, so it guards neither. Leaving it where it stands does not help either. The
black king is \textbf{two} king-moves from $a1$ and the white king is
\textbf{five}, so Black arrives first whatever White does.

\smallskip
So a knight goes, and what is left is K\,+\,N\,+\,N vs K --- which \S\ref{sub:kn-claim} says
White wins only where he mates on the move. Hence a draw, with three knights on
the board and not a thing hanging.}
\end{minipage}
\end{center}

\noindent
That is the whole mechanism, and it explains why the quiet draws are a
\emph{share} that grows while the draws themselves get rarer: a trapped knight
in a corner is a local accident, so the count of such positions grows with the
board far more slowly than the count of positions does --- but among the draws
it comes to dominate the hanging-knight cases, which need Black to be next to a
knight already. It also does not depend on the board at all. The same five
squares are drawn on $15\times15$, $13\times13$, $12\times12$, $10\times10$ and
$8\times8$ --- and at the small sizes the value can be had in a second:

\begin{center}\small
\texttt{./kqk gen -n 8 -{}-endgame knnnk -{}-probe -{}-wk f5 -{}-wp1 a1 -{}-wp2 e5 -{}-wp3 f6 -{}-bk c3}
\end{center}

\noindent
which is the opposite of the two-knight draw of \S\ref{sub:kn-vanish}, where the extra rank was
the \emph{only} thing buying the draw. Both are quiet; one is bought by
geometry that any board has, the other by room that only a big board has.

\subsection{The hardest position is White's own men in his own way}\label{sub:kn-hardest}

The deepest win has the same shape on every board from $8\times8$ up, and it is
not the black king in a corner. It is the \emph{white} king in one, with two of
his own knights standing on the two squares he would use to get out.

\begin{center}
\begin{minipage}{0.30\textwidth}\centering
\bdw{3.1cm}{8}{\pw{0}{0}{\glyphK}\pw{1}{0}{\glyphN}\pw{6}{0}{\glyphN}%
\pw{0}{1}{\glyphN}\pb{5}{1}}\\[2mm]
{\small\color{knnnk}\textbf{$8\times8$} --- white to move,\\mate in 21}
\end{minipage}\hfill
\begin{minipage}{0.30\textwidth}\centering
\bdw{3.1cm}{10}{\pw{0}{0}{\glyphK}\pw{1}{0}{\glyphN}\pw{8}{0}{\glyphN}%
\pw{0}{1}{\glyphN}\pb{7}{1}}\\[2mm]
{\small\color{knnnk}\textbf{$10\times10$} --- white to move,\\mate in 29}
\end{minipage}\hfill
\begin{minipage}{0.30\textwidth}\centering
\bdw{3.1cm}{12}{\pw{0}{0}{\glyphK}\pw{1}{0}{\glyphN}\pw{8}{0}{\glyphN}%
\pw{0}{1}{\glyphN}\pb{7}{1}}\\[2mm]
{\small\color{knnnk}\textbf{$12\times12$} --- white to move,\\mate in 36}
\end{minipage}
\end{center}

\noindent
White king $a1$, knights $b1$ and $a2$ --- the two squares his king needs --- and
the third knight far off on the first rank with the black king beside it. The
worst case is not a defensive resource of Black's at all. It is White having to
spend the opening moves untangling his own pieces before the hunt can start,
and then walk the whole board to reach the king. That the same arrangement is
the worst on every board from $8\times8$ to $14\times14$ is what says it is a
structure rather than an accident of one board --- and on $13\times13$ and
$14\times14$ it is not merely the same shape but the same five squares,
wK\,$a1$, wN\,$b1$, wN\,$a2$, wN\,$l1$, bK\,$k2$, at 81 plies and then 89.

