\end{center}

\needspace{10\baselineskip}
\section{How far any of this can be trusted}\label{sec:trust}

\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lp{0.615\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Bellman re-derivation} & Every entry recomputed from its successors with
an independent forward move generator. $n \le 24$ for KQK and KRK, $n \le 14$
for KBBK and KBNK, $n \le 16$ for KQKR, $n \le 17$ for KQKK, $n \le 11$ for
KQKK under capture rules --- there, all four of its tables --- $n \le 16$ for
KNK and KNNK, and $n \le 10$ for KNNNK, whose checker carries its own copy of
the conversion into KNNK rather than sharing the solver's. Larger boards on a
deterministic sample. \\[2mm]
\textbf{Unreduced reference solver} & The same endgame solved with \emph{no}
symmetry at all --- one byte per placement, one thread, and by a different
algorithm --- then compared position by position over every placement on the
board. 33\,030\,144 placements at $n=8$ for each bare-king endgame; every one
of the $n^8$ \emph{ordered} placements up to $n=6$ for KQKK, which checks its
king-triple index at the placements it can only reach through a symmetry, and
all four capture-rule tables likewise; and every ordered placement to $n=7$ for
KNNNK --- 530\,832\,288 of them --- which is what exercises the unordered-triple
index. Both bugs the capture rules cost were
found this way and both were in the \emph{checker}: a reference solver that
sweeps until nothing changes stops too early on a table that converts, and one
that takes a win as soon as it is computable records the first win found rather
than the shortest. This
is what catches indexing bugs, which a self-consistency check structurally
cannot. KQKR needs signed entries and twice the memory, which stops it at
$n=6$ --- hence the row below. \\[2mm]
\textbf{Published $8\times8$ figures} & 462 canonical king pairs, 364 KQK
checkmates, 872 stalemates, 22\,176 queen-\emph{en-prise} draws --- all
reproduced exactly, and KQKR's mate in 35. The KRK, KBBK and KBNK columns were
then \emph{derived} by the brute force rather than looked up. \textbf{KQKK has
no published figure to check against}, being a variant rather than a standard
endgame: its evidence is the two cross-checks above, the two below, and
positions worked out by hand and then looked up. \textbf{KNNNK's $8\times8$
depth of mate in 21 is likewise not checked against any published figure}; what
it does have is the unreduced solver agreeing over all $8^{10}$ ordered
placements at $n \le 7$. The 120 KNNK mates on $8\times8$, by contrast, were
counted by a throwaway script written before any of the C++ existed, and the
two agree. \\[2mm]
\textbf{Naive move generator, naive index} & KQKR and KQKK, and for the same
reason in both cases --- the brute force runs out of memory long before the
interesting boards. Each move generator is compared against one that walks
every ray a square at a time; KQKR's index against the definition of a $D_4$
orbit and KQKK's against the requirement that a table value be constant on a
symmetry class, including under exchanging the two black kings, which are
indistinguishable. Random placements to $n=16$ and $n=12$. \\[2mm]
\textbf{A guess not made} & KNNNK is the one endgame here whose sub-table could
have been assumed instead of built, and the assumption would have been wrong:
``two knights cannot mate'' is false as stated. The solver refuses to run
without a KNNK table attached, and the test suite exercises that refusal rather
than trusting it. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\begin{result}[title={The bug that mattered}]{ink}
KBNK first reported mate in \textbf{35} on $8\times8$. The textbook value is 33,
and that discrepancy is what exposed it.

Phase~B treated ``the black king steps onto a white piece'' as a capture, hence
a draw. With one white piece that is sound --- squares next to the white king
are already excluded, so nothing can defend it. With two it is wrong. And it
\emph{hid}: in KBBK two bishops can only defend each other along a shared
diagonal, hence only when same-coloured, and those positions are drawn anyway
--- so the bug was live in KBBK for as long as KBBK existed and changed not one
entry. A bishop and knight defend each other freely, and there it cost 2\,816
lost positions on a $5\times5$ board.

\smallskip
Both the verifier and the unreduced solver flagged the same positions,
independently. The wrong answer was a plausible-looking number, not a crash ---
which is the argument for keeping both.
\end{result}

